WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:01.470
Okay.

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:07.220
We've been covering the state level proposals
to make abortions almost or completely illegal.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:12.210
Yesterday we talked about Alabama, no abortions,
other than exceptions for the health of the

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.740
mother on the program.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.960
Tomorrow I'm going to talk about Missouri.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.170
Uh, and I've already explained the sort of
underlying idea here, which is that you've

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:24.460
got a right wing Supreme Court now thanks
to Donald Trump more right wing than any court

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.449
we've had in a long time.

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:30.810
So the strategy is get some crazy bill passed
at the state level.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:35.160
It will be immediately appealed and challenged
and more than likely because it's so whacked

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.070
out and in many ways would conflict with Roe
v Wade.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.931
It is likely to be struck down by a lower
court, appeal it all the way up to the Supreme

00:00:42.931 --> 00:00:47.180
Court and then hopefully get a decision from
this very conservative supreme court that

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:53.470
will allow a national push back, uh, on abortion
regulations everywhere.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:01:00.090
Um, there's a really good piece in vox that
explains that this is not just like a pipe

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:07.570
dream for the religious right, that this is
Roe v Wade in spirit and in name is in danger

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:12.710
because of this what I, what I called yesterday,
a very clever strategy by the religious right.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:17.580
But what I want to talk about today is that
even if Roe v Wade stands legally speaking,

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:24.580
if it stays enacted in force, the spirit of
Robie Roe v Wade is in danger either way because

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:30.820
you can undo the effect of Roe versus Wade
without actually undoing roe versus wade in

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:31.909
and of itself.

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:37.190
And that is uh, sort of the premise of these
really extreme anti abortion bills that have

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:41.840
passed in 2019 four, five six depending on
how you're counting.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.490
And at some point something is very likely
to get to the supreme court.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:47.490
Okay.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.350
That won't by definition get Roe v Wade overturned.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:55.080
And there are more and more analysts that
are pointing out astutely that overturning

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.711
Roe v Wade may not even actually be the goal
of these hands.

00:01:58.711 --> 00:02:02.790
High choice activists and it might not even
be the best thing for the anti choice movement.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:10.229
The idea being if Roe v Wade stays as the
law, that emboldens and empowers the activism

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:15.450
of the Anti Choice Movement to say Roe v Wade
is still there, but we're working at the state

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.340
level to stop all abortion.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.060
They can keep raising money around the issue
and you can do a lot of damage to abortion

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:25.450
rights without actually overturning Roe v
Wade itself.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:34.410
Roe v Wade in 73, um, at the federal level
said abortion is legal up to a 22 to 24 weeks

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.739
in most cases.

00:02:35.739 --> 00:02:41.180
And so the problem that a lot of states run
up against when they try to pass more restrictive

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:47.010
laws than that is that they conflict with
federal supremacy and Roe v Wade.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:52.060
So that's why lower courts will typically
strike them down and the supreme court is

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:56.560
rarely going to hear them because it's pretty
clear that they conflict with Roe v Wade.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.040
The idea here is you've got a particularly
conservative Supreme Court, if you can word

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:08.849
one of these bills in a way that may be some
element of it or along some very narrow legal

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:10.209
interpretation.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:15.430
The law would be okay at the state level or
a part of the law would be okay at the state

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:22.800
level, you could get a ruling from this very
conservative supreme court that will without

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.720
overturning Roe v Wade in and of itself create
a framework or a sort of guide to how at the

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.860
state level you can pass something that would
be more restrictive than Roe v Wade.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:39.459
The um, other practical matter, uh, that would
point to this being a better strategy than

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:44.640
just trying to get Roe v Wade overturned,
is that even the antiabortion Supreme Court

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:50.300
justices may not want to attach their names
to overturning Roe v Wade.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:55.840
So whether Roe v Wade stays or goes in name,
the idea here is that the principles of roe

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.740
versus Wade can be dismantled by these endless
little cuts that could come as soon as one

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.550
of these state abortion bans gets to the supreme
court and gets a favorable ruling even in

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:07.550
some sense.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:15.250
And then they can make a states be allowed
to have much more leeway in deciding what

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.459
to do about abortion.

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:22.240
And maybe the most important thing to remind
you about is that with lots of, uh, similarly

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.919
to lots of other political issues that are
great for angering people and for raising

00:04:26.919 --> 00:04:28.639
money and for making noise.

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:34.250
Um, uh, uh, but not necessarily representative
of the national opinion.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.370
Most of the American people are not on the
antiabortion side.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.729
And we have sort of two important data points
on this.

00:04:39.729 --> 00:04:44.070
Number one, for the first time ever, a few
years ago, support for abortion in what's

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.350
called most cases.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:49.560
So obviously there are particular cases where
you do get a majority of the country say,

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.040
am I am against abortions in that particular
situation.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.340
Um, in most cases, more than half the country
supports abortion.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.381
And that is a, a number that was only first
achieved a couple of years ago, so was support

00:05:03.381 --> 00:05:04.800
for abortion has been rising.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.330
But even if you look at the individual state
level, there is not a single state where a

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:13.540
total abortion ban has even 25% support.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:19.620
We're putting on the screen a data analysis
by data for progress from cooperative congressional

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.389
election studies data.

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:27.650
So understand that states are passing almost
total abortion bans, even though the people

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:33.430
in not a single state support that at even
a rate of 25%.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:37.610
The problem is that abortion is a really strong
single issue.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:38.610
Topic.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.280
Guns, we've talked about guns.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.669
People are very, uh, uh, a partisan and extreme.

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:48.960
And by by extreme, I don't mean extreme on
the political spectrum, but very ingrained

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.490
views about guns.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:55.750
But it doesn't seem like guns are nearly as
much of a voting issue as other issues.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.020
Abortion is a really big voting issue.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:03.860
Donald Trump has lived a life completely antithetical
to evangelical Christianity.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:09.009
Stated principles stated being a key word,
but in the end, evangelicals voted for Trump.

00:06:09.009 --> 00:06:10.009
Why?

00:06:10.009 --> 00:06:14.640
Because Trump now claims to be against abortion
and uh, Hillary Clinton is pro choice and

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.150
that actually is a big voting motivator for
people in lots of parts of the country.

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:25.560
So Roe v Wade is in serious danger, even if
the law in and of itself remains in force.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.039
I told you exactly the way that it would happen
two years ago.

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:34.650
It is happening and if there's any wakeup
call here, it's, we cannot let Donald Trump

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.760
